  "answer": "I'll add an optional manufacturer website that is stored, accepted on create and update, returned in manufacturer JSON, and rejected when the URL scheme is wrong. First I'm loading the feature playbook and the principles that apply.The website belongs on the manufacturer itself. Create and update reject a bad URL in the private API before anything is saved, and every manufacturer JSON path copies the same field.Manufacturers now have an optional `website` URL. It is stored on the manufacturer row, accepted on private create and update, and returned as `website` on manufacturer detail, manufacturer lists, and the manufacturer nested in a product. A value that does not start with `http://` or `https://` raises HTTP 400 and is not saved. Laziness Protocol kept this as one field on the existing manufacturer types. Model the Domain treats the website as an attribute of the manufacturer. Boundary Discipline rejects a bad URL in the private API before anything is written.\n\nVERIFICATION: {\"ran\": [\"mvn -pl sm-core-model,sm-shop-model,sm-shop -am compile -DskipTests -q\"], \"result\": \"not-run\", \"could_not_verify\": \"HTTP 400, database persistence, and product JSON were not exercised against a running server.\", \"missing\": \"A manufacturer API integration test, or a documented way to boot the private API against H2.\"}The manufacturer walkthrough from [Explain manufacturer API](57a1ebb9-94fb-4d2a-ab1a-423504dcf9de) matches the change already in the tree. The website sits on the core manufacturer and the shared shop model, and it is copied on write, on detail and list reads, on the product mapper, and in the older product populator. No further code change is needed.",
